    {
      "id": "4bfcpi4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.2 Warkari and Bhakti Movement in Maharashtra"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/A2qp5r_wZmwzrT03S_Bw3UiEC8t7_HiUk3FsVY2MlqXE2F27oCoUiQgL18OPshZcSa2pSW2hpVPNYsp4ynBHa4ujlVrDQnllCe5Nsl_NqJUJHyzhPPANx_rbidJ-dFC5IHaZe_M5rYJ8TIRL3uz87rh43H0tqUdS0_OqT63q7ezDy1Y_CBuHmKhEyVDOoV71?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/MAHARSHTRA GS/images/image_6buigmt.jpeg\"/>The Varkari (Warkari) sect is a devotional movement centered around the worship of Vithoba (Vitthal / Pandurang) at Pandharpur, Solapur district. It is one of the most important socio-religious movements in Maharashtra's history.",
      "id": "fjbhmn9"
    },
    {
      "id": "hy9e9q0",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Pilgrimage called Wari to Pandharpur twice a year (Ashadhi and Kartiki Ekadashi)",
        "Dnyaneshwar, Namdev, Eknath, Tukaram, Mirabai, Chokhamela are key saints",
        "Dnyaneshwar is considered the founder of the Varkari movement (Alandi, Pune)",
        "Tukaram's Dehu is the starting point of the major Wari to Pandharpur",
        "Chokhamela: Dalit saint; emphasized equality in Bhakti",
        "Muktabai: Sister of Dnyaneshwar; also a recognized saint"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xzjk29x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.3 Festivals of Maharashtra"
    },
    {
      "id": "8h82hsd",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Festival</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>When / Month</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Significance</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ganesh Chaturthi</p>",
            "<p>Bhadrapada (Aug-Sep)</p>",
            "<p>10-day festival; Lokmanya Tilak started public celebrations in 1893; Lalbaugcha Raja (most famous idol in Mumbai)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Gudi Padwa</p>",
            "<p>Chaitra Shukla Pratipada (Mar-Apr)</p>",
            "<p>Maharashtrian New Year; Gudi (flag) hoisted outside homes</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Navratri / Dasara</p>",
            "<p>Ashwin (Sep-Oct)</p>",
            "<p>Nine nights of worship; Kolhapur and Tuljapur important</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Diwali (Faral)</p>",
            "<p>Kartik (Oct-Nov)</p>",
            "<p>Festival of lights; Maharashtrians make Faral (special sweets/snacks)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Holi (Shimga)</p>",
            "<p>Phalgun (Feb-Mar)</p>",
            "<p>Called Shimga in Maharashtra</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ashadhi Ekadashi</p>",
            "<p>Ashadha (Jun-Jul)</p>",
            "<p>Wari pilgrimage to Pandharpur; Vitthal worship</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kartiki Ekadashi</p>",
            "<p>Kartik (Oct-Nov)</p>",
            "<p>Return Wari pilgrimage to Pandharpur</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Makar Sankranti</p>",
            "<p>January 14</p>",
            "<p>Kite flying; tilgul (sesame &amp; jaggery) exchange</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shivaji Jayanti</p>",
            "<p>19 February</p>",
            "<p>Birthday of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Maharashtra Din</p>",
            "<p>1 May</p>",
            "<p>Maharashtra State Formation Day</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ja1ud95",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.4 Classical Dance Forms"
    },
    {
      "id": "0yaev0z",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Lavani</strong></p>",
            "<p>Most popular folk dance-music form; erotic and devotional themes; Tamasha is the theater form associated with it; Baithakichi and Phadkichi Lavani are two types</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Tamasha</strong></p>",
            "<p>Folk theater form combining Lavani, Vag (play), and Gaana; associated with nomadic communities</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Koli Dance</strong></p>",
            "<p>Fisher folk dance of Koli community in Konkan and Mumbai region</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Dhangari Gaja</strong></p>",
            "<p>Dance of Dhangar (shepherd) community; Vitthal worship theme</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Gondhali</strong></p>",
            "<p>Ritual dance form worshipping goddess; performed during Navratri</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Powada</strong></p>",
            "<p>Ballad tradition; glorifies heroes like Shivaji; sung by Shahirs (bards)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Lezim</strong></p>",
            "<p>Group exercise dance; performed during Ganesh festival processions</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Bharud</strong></p>",
            "<p>Devotional song-drama; associated with Eknath; social satire through religion</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "69bvaqt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.5 UNESCO World Heritage Sites in Maharashtra"
    },
    {
      "id": "38j1kxr",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ajanta Caves</strong></p>",
            "<p>Aurangabad district; 30 Buddhist cave monuments (paintings and sculptures); 2nd century BCE to 6th century CE; UNESCO WHS since 1983</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ellora Caves</strong></p>",
            "<p>Aurangabad district; 34 caves (Buddhist, Hindu, Jain); Kailasa Temple (Cave 16) – largest rock-cut monolithic temple in the world; UNESCO WHS since 1983</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Elephanta Caves</strong></p>",
            "<p>Elephanta Island near Mumbai; Shiva sculptures; UNESCO WHS since 1987</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (CST)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Mumbai; Victorian Gothic and Indian architecture; UNESCO WHS since 2004</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Victorian Gothic and Art Deco Ensembles</strong></p>",
            "<p>Mumbai; declared UNESCO WHS in 2018</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Western Ghats (Sahyadri)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Includes parts of Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka, Kerala; UNESCO WHS since 2012</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
